// ServiceCompanions — what the compiler synthesizes beside a SERVICE, and the rules that decide there is
// one (SV1 wave 2). Designed in `SERVICES_DESIGN.md` §"Recommended design".
//
// ⭐⭐ **A `spawn T.create()` ANYWHERE IN THE PROGRAM MAKES `T` A SERVICE.** There is no `service` keyword
// and no member keyword: a service is an ordinary `type`, and its message surface is exactly its
// `export`/`public` INSTANCE methods (`StructLayout.messageMethodNames`). Two companion types are minted
// beside it, under the DOTTED names an author writes:
//
//   • **`T.request`** — a union, one variant per message in declaration order, behind the compiler's own
//     `__shutdown` at variant 0. A message's payload is its parameter list, plus a trailing `__reply`
//     integer when the message has a reply to deliver.
//   • **`T.handle`** — a one-field struct holding the mailbox pointer. A REAL 8-byte box on
//     `managedFileLayout`'s shape, deliberately: moves, E3102, struct fields, arrays and the drop cascade
//     then all reach it by construction rather than through a special case at each door.
//
// ⚠⚠ **WHY THE SYNTHESIS IS SILENT AND THE REFUSALS ARE THE PARSER'S.** Everything in this file runs inside
// `queryProgramSignatures`, which is MEMOIZED and which `Parser.foldDeclaredSignaturesInto` states outright
// is **not a validator**. A diagnostic reported from here would be reported on a memo MISS and silently
// skipped on a HIT — the exact trap `ProgramSignatures.evaluateInitializers` records for itself (*"It
// reports NOTHING. A defective initializer is recorded on its declaration and raised, with a position, by
// the real parse when it reaches that line"*). So this file DECIDES and the parser SPEAKS: `Parser.parseSpawn`
// asks `classifyServiceTarget` and `classifyServiceRoster` at the `spawn` the author wrote, and raises the
// refusal there, positioned. That also satisfies the design's own requirement — *"the diagnostic must name
// the spawn site that made the type a service"* — by construction rather than by carrying a site around: the
// diagnostic IS at the spawn site.
//
// ⚠ **THE COMPANIONS ARE SYNTHESIZED FOR A VALID TARGET EVEN WHEN ITS ROSTER IS REFUSED**, and that is
// deliberate. A program whose service takes an untransferable message parameter is refused at its `spawn`;
// if the companions were withheld as well, a `Calc.handle` named in another file would ALSO report
// `Unknown type`, and the author would read a cascade instead of the one finding. Only a target that cannot
// HAVE companions — a generic type, whose pair would have to be per-instantiation — is skipped.

// ⭐⭐ **ONE SPAWN TARGET AS THE DISCOVERY WALK READS IT — a TYPE and the FACTORY named on it.** Both halves
// are needed to decide whether the type is a service: `spawn Calc.bump()` names a declared type and is not a
// spawn at all, because `bump` is an instance method rather than a static factory returning `Calc`.
module type ServiceSpawnCandidate
	export let typeName as ByteArray
	export let member as ByteArray

	export static function create(typeName ByteArray, member ByteArray) returns ServiceSpawnCandidate
		return Self{typeName: typeName, member: member}
	end 'create'
end 'ServiceSpawnCandidate'

module typealias ServiceSpawnCandidateArray = Array with ServiceSpawnCandidate

// ⭐⭐ **WHY `spawn <T>.<member>(…)` IS OR IS NOT A SERVICE START.** One verdict, TWO askers — the sweep,
// which mints the companions for the accepting arm, and `Parser.parseSpawn`, which turns every refusing arm
// into its positioned diagnostic. Asking it twice in two spellings is how the compiler comes to mint a
// companion for a target it then refuses, or to refuse one it minted for.
module union ServiceTargetVerdict
	// `<member>` is a static factory of the declared type `<T>` returning `<T>`. `<T>` is a service.
	startsAService
	// No file declares `type <T>`. (An `enum`/`union` lands here too: a service is a `type`, because its
	// state is what the message loop owns and a union's value is its own tag.)
	typeIsNotAStruct
	// `<T>` is declared and nothing declares `<T>.<member>`.
	memberIsNotDeclared
	// `<T>.<member>` is an INSTANCE method. `spawn` calls its factory DIRECTLY — there is no service yet for
	// a message to reach — so the target must be a `static`.
	memberTakesAReceiver
	// `<T>.<member>` is a static whose recorded return type is not `<T>`'s own struct box (`void` included).
	// A factory that does not hand one back has produced no state for the loop to own.
	//
	// ⚠ **A BUILTIN-BACKED TYPE LANDS HERE, AND THE ARM'S WORDS ARE CHOSEN SO THAT IS NOT A LIE.** MEASURED:
	// `stdlib/String.maxon:119` declares `public static function from (bytes ByteArray) returns String`, and
	// its RECORDED return type is the builtin `string` tag rather than `structRef(String)` — the parser types
	// a `String` return that way while `declaredSlotType` resolves the same NAME to a struct box, which is a
	// pre-existing asymmetry between two doors and not this rung's to settle. So `spawn String.from(b"x")` is
	// refused here, correctly (a builtin's value is not a declared struct's box for a message loop to own) —
	// and the sentence therefore says what the TEST is rather than asserting the author's factory returns
	// something it does not.
	memberDoesNotReturnTheType
	// `type <T> uses …` — the companions are monomorphic by construction (one union, one handle struct), so
	// a generic service would need a pair per instantiation. E2015: not built, not refused forever.
	typeIsGeneric
end 'ServiceTargetVerdict'

// ⭐ **WHY A SERVICE'S MESSAGE ROSTER CANNOT BE DISPATCHED.** Split from `ServiceTargetVerdict` because the
// two answer about different declarations — the target is about the FACTORY, this is about the messages —
// and because the companions are minted for a valid target whose roster is refused (see this file's header).
module union ServiceRosterVerdict
	// Every message can become one variant of the request union.
	rosterIsDispatchable
	// A message declared more than once. One variant carries one payload shape, so an overloaded message has
	// no single shape to become — and the dispatch `match` would have no arm to pick between them. E2015.
	messageIsOverloaded(methodName ByteArray, declarationCount FieldIndex)
	// A message parameter of a type that cannot cross to another green thread. `noun` is what it IS, so the
	// sentence reads as the reason rather than as a spelling (see `untransferableParameterNoun`).
	messageParameterIsNotTransferable(methodName ByteArray, paramName ByteArray, noun String)
end 'ServiceRosterVerdict'

// The pair minted for one service — returned together because they are built from one reading of the type
// and registered by one act. Two separate builders would be two walks of the same roster.
module type ServiceCompanions
	export let request as EnumLayout
	export let handle as StructLayout

	export static function create(request EnumLayout, handle StructLayout) returns ServiceCompanions
		return Self{request: request, handle: handle}
	end 'create'
end 'ServiceCompanions'

// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS A SPAWN TARGET? — the whole rule, in one place.** The order of the arms is the order the
// questions can be ASKED: a member cannot be classified before its type is known to be a struct, and a
// generic type is refused ahead of its member because the refusal is about the type either way.
//
// ⚠ **THE RETURN TYPE IS MATCHED AS `structRef` OR `named`, AND BOTH ARE REQUIRED AT THIS MOMENT.** The
// sweep resolves `returns Self` to a `structRef` from the tokens alone, but a factory written
// `returns Calc` records a bare `named` until `ProgramSignatures.rewriteRecordedReturnTypes` re-tags it —
// and that runs LATER in `queryProgramSignatures` than the synthesis this classification feeds (which must
// precede `allFilesFolded = true`, so the companions are ordinary layouts to every normalization after it).
// Accepting only `structRef` here would make `returns Self` a service and `returns Calc` not one, which is
// the same declaration written two ways.
module function classifyServiceTarget(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeName ByteArray, member ByteArray) returns ServiceTargetVerdict
	var declared = try serviceLayoutOf(signatures, typeName: typeName) otherwise return ServiceTargetVerdict.typeIsNotAStruct

	if declared.typeParamCount() > 0 'generic'
		return ServiceTargetVerdict.typeIsGeneric
	end 'generic'

	let decls = signatures.overloadedDeclsOf(qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: member))
	if decls.count() == 0 'noSuchMember'
		return ServiceTargetVerdict.memberIsNotDeclared
	end 'noSuchMember'

	// ⚠ The FIRST declaration decides, and an overloaded FACTORY is therefore read off its first member. A
	// spawn picks its overload the way any call does (`SemanticCheck.resolveOverloadedCalls`), which is a
	// pass this sweep runs before — but every member of a factory's overload set agrees about the two facts
	// asked here, because they are what make it a factory at all: a `static` beside an instance method of
	// one name is already refused (`requireOverloadableName`), and a set disagreeing about the return type
	// is E3005 at its own declaration.
	let factory = try decls.get(0) otherwise panic("classifyServiceTarget: overloadedDeclsOf('{String.from(typeName)}.{String.from(member)}') answered {decls.count()} declarations and get(0) failed")
	if factory.takesReceiver 'anInstanceMethod'
		return ServiceTargetVerdict.memberTakesAReceiver
	end 'anInstanceMethod'

	if not serviceReturnTypeNames(signatures, returned: factory.returnType, typeName: typeName) 'returnsSomethingElse'
		return ServiceTargetVerdict.memberDoesNotReturnTheType
	end 'returnsSomethingElse'

	return ServiceTargetVerdict.startsAService
end 'classifyServiceTarget'

// Does the recorded return type name `typeName` itself? See `classifyServiceTarget` for why BOTH id-bearing
// tags are accepted at this point in the sweep.
function serviceReturnTypeNames(signatures ProgramSignatures, returned MaxonReturnType, typeName ByteArray) returns bool
	match returned 'returns'
		void then return false
		value(inner) then return serviceTypeNames(signatures, t: inner, typeName: typeName)
	end 'returns'
end 'serviceReturnTypeNames'

// Does the id-bearing type `t` name `typeName`? A type carrying no name at all (`void` has been excluded, so
// what remains is `int`, `bool`, a container instance) names nothing and answers `false`.
function serviceTypeNames(signatures ProgramSignatures, t MaxonType, typeName ByteArray) returns bool
	let tag = maxonTypeTag(t)
	if tag != ValueTypeTag.structRef and tag != ValueTypeTag.named 'namesNoType'
		return false
	end 'namesNoType'

	return serviceIndexTypeName(signatures, id: typeNameIdOf(t)).equals(typeName)
end 'serviceTypeNames'

// A type-name id resolved against the SIGNATURE INDEX's own interner — the table every id reaching this file
// was minted in (`ProgramSignatures.foldFile` remaps a file's ids into it). Resolving one anywhere else is
// OPEN #56, whose two measured wrong answers `indexTypeNames`' header records.
function serviceIndexTypeName(signatures ProgramSignatures, id TypeNameId) returns ByteArray
	return try signatures.indexTypeNames().nameOf(id) otherwise panic("serviceIndexTypeName: type-name id {id} is not in the signature index's interner — every id a swept declaration carries was remapped into it at `foldFile`")
end 'serviceIndexTypeName'

// ⭐ **THE ONE LOOKUP DOOR — because five of them was five copies of one `match`, and every caller past the
// first also needs the LAYOUT rather than one fact off it.** `structTypes` is keyed by NAME, so asking it
// once per message (the roster walk did) hashes a string to re-find a record the caller already holds — the
// measured cost `ProgramSignatures.fieldTypeOf`'s header records for exactly this shape.
//
// It THROWS rather than answering a default: "no such type" is the target classification's own first
// verdict, and every other caller has already established the declaration.
function serviceLayoutOf(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeName ByteArray) returns StructLayout throws MapError
	match signatures.structOf(typeName) 'lookup'
		undeclared then throw MapError.keyNotFound
		declared(layout) then return layout
	end 'lookup'
end 'serviceLayoutOf'

// The layout of a type a caller has already classified a spawn TARGET. Split from the door above so the
// message names the invariant rather than repeating the `otherwise panic` at six call sites.
function spawnTargetLayoutOf(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeName ByteArray) returns StructLayout
	return try serviceLayoutOf(signatures, typeName: typeName) otherwise panic("spawnTargetLayoutOf: nothing declares `type {String.from(typeName)}` — every caller has already classified it a spawn target, which requires the declaration")
end 'spawnTargetLayoutOf'

// ⭐⭐ **CAN EVERY MESSAGE BECOME A VARIANT? — the roster's own rule, asked in declaration order so the
// FIRST offending message is the one named.** A stable answer matters: this decides the text of a
// diagnostic that fires at a `spawn` in a different file from the method it names.
module function classifyServiceRoster(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeName ByteArray) returns ServiceRosterVerdict
	var layout = spawnTargetLayoutOf(signatures, typeName: typeName)
	for i in 0 upto layout.messageMethodCount() 'eachMessage'
		let methodName = layout.messageMethodAt(i)
		let decls = signatures.overloadedDeclsOf(qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: methodName))
		if decls.count() > 1 'overloaded'
			return ServiceRosterVerdict.messageIsOverloaded(methodName, declarationCount: decls.count())
		end 'overloaded'

		let decl = try decls.get(0) otherwise panic("classifyServiceRoster: '{String.from(typeName)}.{String.from(methodName)}' is on the roster the declaration sweep built from its own declaration, so `overloadedDeclsOf` cannot be empty")
		for p in 0 upto decl.paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
			let declared = try decl.paramTypes.get(p) otherwise panic("classifyServiceRoster: paramTypes.get OOB at {p} below count {decl.paramTypes.count()} for '{String.from(typeName)}.{String.from(methodName)}'")
			let noun = untransferableParameterNoun(signatures, declared: declared, declFilePath: layout.declFilePath)
			if not noun.isEmpty() 'cannotCross'
				return ServiceRosterVerdict.messageParameterIsNotTransferable(methodName, paramName: serviceParamName(decl, index: p), noun: noun)
			end 'cannotCross'
		end 'eachParam'
	end 'eachMessage'

	return ServiceRosterVerdict.rosterIsDispatchable
end 'classifyServiceRoster'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A PARAMETER *IS* WHEN IT CANNOT CROSS A GREEN THREAD, or EMPTY when it can.** A NOUN and not a
// spelling, because the sentence has to say why: `'p' is a Promise` is the reason, where `'p' is
// 'Promise with (int, E)'` is a rendering the author can already read off their own declaration.
//
// ⚠ **THE RULE IS THE PLAIN REFCOUNT'S, NOT A STYLE.** Since EC10 a refcount step is a plain load/add/store
// (`Runtime/MmRuntime.maxon`, `emitAdjustRefcount`) because the language guarantees one green thread per box.
// A send MOVES its arguments, which keeps that guarantee — but only for a value that HAS one owner and one
// meaning on the far side. Each shape below fails that for its own reason:
//   • a **Promise** is a green-thread handle owned by the awaiting thread; sending one hands a second thread
//     a wait token whose completer is scheduled on the first (`E3100` linear await keys on the binding);
//   • a **function value** is a bare code pointer, and a CAPTURING one reaches its environment block —
//     which is `shared`, i.e. a box with a second referent by construction (P1.5 escape);
//   • an **interface value** is a fat pointer whose witness half is `.rdata` and whose value half is
//     released through `__drop_existential`; the request union's payload cascade cannot express the pair;
//   • an **opaque type parameter** has no layout at the send, so the sender cannot know whether it is even
//     managed — and a generic service is refused outright one rule up.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED OF THE RESOLVED TYPE.** A parameter written `Promise with (int, E)` is recorded by the
// sweep as a bare `named` naming its alias, so testing the swept type directly would let every aliased
// spelling through. `declaredSlotType` is the one door that resolves a swept slot type, and it is the same
// door the payload build uses — so the check and the payload cannot come to disagree about what a parameter
// is.
function untransferableParameterNoun(signatures ProgramSignatures, declared MaxonType, declFilePath FilePath) returns String
	let resolved = signatures.declaredSlotType(declared, into: signatures.indexTypeNames(), slotScopeFilePath: declFilePath)
	if typeIsPromiseStorage(signatures, t: resolved) 'aPromise'
		return "a Promise, which is a green-thread handle its awaiter owns"
	end 'aPromise'

	match maxonTypeTag(resolved) 'tag'
		function then return "a function value, whose captured environment is a box a second thread would share"
		interfaceRef then return InterfaceParameterNoun
		// ⚠ **UNREACHABLE TODAY AND STILL THE RIGHT ANSWER.** Only a GENERIC type's method can declare a
		// type-parameter-typed parameter, and a generic type is refused as a spawn target one rule earlier
		// (`ServiceTargetVerdict.typeIsGeneric`) — so no roster reaching here holds one. It answers rather
		// than returning "" because the day a service can be generic, "transferable" would be the wrong
		// default for the one shape whose layout the send site cannot know.
		typeParameter then return "an opaque type parameter, whose layout is not known at the send"
		// ⭐⭐ **A BARE `named` MAY STILL BE AN INTERFACE, AND THIS IS WHERE THAT IS ASKED (MEASURED).**
		// `ProgramSignatures.normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes` re-tags a swept `named` that denotes an interface —
		// but only in a struct's FIELD column and in the RETURN column, never in `OverloadedDecl.paramTypes`.
		// `declaredSlotType` does not close the gap either: its `interfaceType` arm deliberately THROWS (see
		// `denotedSlotType`, which argues that arm must stay a throw), so a parameter written `s Shape`
		// arrives here as `named` and was silently accepted. Measured on a two-declaration program before this
		// arm: `export function measure(s Shape)` on a spawned type compiled straight through to the wave-3
		// E2015 with no transferability finding at all.
		named then return declaredInterfaceParameterNoun(signatures, resolved: resolved)
		// Every remaining tag names a value with ONE owner and a layout the send site knows: a scalar, an
		// exit code, a struct box, a String, a container instance, a character. Each MOVES.
		boolean to float then return ""
		exitCode to string then return ""
		genericInstance to character then return ""
	end 'tag'
end 'untransferableParameterNoun'

// The noun for a value held at an interface type — one string, because the two arms that reach that verdict
// (an `interfaceRef` the parse minted, a bare `named` the sweep left) are one finding about one declaration.
let InterfaceParameterNoun = "a value held at an interface type, which is a fat pointer released through a witness the request union cannot carry"

// Does this bare `named` name a declared INTERFACE? Asked of the same registry the field/return normalization
// asks (`ProgramSignatures.declaredFormOf` reads `interfaces`), so a parameter and a field of one interface
// type cannot come to different answers.
//
// A name no registry declares is E3011 at the declaration itself and is not this check's finding.
function declaredInterfaceParameterNoun(signatures ProgramSignatures, resolved MaxonType) returns String
	let name = serviceIndexTypeName(signatures, id: typeNameIdOf(resolved))
	let form = try signatures.declaredFormOf(name) otherwise return ""
	return InterfaceParameterNoun if form == TypeDeclKind.interfaceType else ""
end 'declaredInterfaceParameterNoun'

// A declaration's parameter name at `index`. `OverloadedDecl`'s two columns are source-aligned with the
// receiver excluded, so this index is the one `paramTypes` was read at.
function serviceParamName(decl OverloadedDecl, index ParamCount) returns ByteArray
	return try decl.paramNames.get(index) otherwise panic("serviceParamName: index {index} is out of range for a declaration carrying {decl.paramNames.count()} parameter name(s) against {decl.paramTypes.count()} type(s) — the two columns of an `OverloadedDecl` are written by one act and are source-aligned")
end 'serviceParamName'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE SENTENCE THAT TEACHES THE FORM — written once and quoted by every refusal that needs it.**
// The single most valuable thing a `spawn` diagnostic can say is what the one legal shape IS: there is
// exactly one, and an author who has just written the wrong one is exactly the reader who needs it.
let SpawnTeachingSentence = "`spawn` starts a SERVICE from a static factory of a declared type that returns that type, e.g. `spawn Calc.create()`"

// ⭐⭐ **THE WORDS FOR EVERY REFUSING TARGET VERDICT — beside the rule that chose them.** `ParseError`
// carries the rendered sentence rather than the facts precisely so that this stays true: a message assembled
// at the diagnostic mapper would be one rule stated in two files.
//
// ⚠ It answers for the REFUSING arms only. `startsAService` reaching here would mean a caller refused a
// target this classification accepted, which is a compiler bug and not a sentence.
module function serviceTargetRefusalMessage(verdict ServiceTargetVerdict, typeName ByteArray, member ByteArray) returns String
	let written = "`spawn {String.from(typeName)}.{String.from(member)}(…)`"
	match verdict 'why'
		startsAService then panic("serviceTargetRefusalMessage: asked for the words of an ACCEPTED spawn target — `{String.from(typeName)}.{String.from(member)}` starts a service, and the caller refuses only the other arms")
		typeIsNotAStruct then return "{written} does not start a service: nothing declares `type {String.from(typeName)}`. {SpawnTeachingSentence}"
		memberIsNotDeclared then return "{written} does not start a service: `type {String.from(typeName)}` declares no `{String.from(member)}`. {SpawnTeachingSentence}"
		memberTakesAReceiver then return "{written} does not start a service: `{String.from(typeName)}.{String.from(member)}` is an INSTANCE method, and a `spawn` calls its factory directly — there is no service yet for a message to reach. {SpawnTeachingSentence}"
		memberDoesNotReturnTheType then return "{written} does not start a service: `{String.from(typeName)}.{String.from(member)}` does not hand back a `{String.from(typeName)}` the service can own — a service's state is the BOX of a declared `type`, and this factory's recorded return type is not one. {SpawnTeachingSentence}"
		typeIsGeneric then return "{written} — `type {String.from(typeName)}` is generic, and a service's companions (`{String.from(serviceRequestCompanionName(typeName))}`, `{String.from(serviceHandleCompanionName(typeName))}`) are monomorphic: ONE union and ONE handle struct per service, not one pair per instantiation. Spawn a non-generic type, or declare a concrete wrapper around the generic one"
	end 'why'
end 'serviceTargetRefusalMessage'

// ⭐ **THE WORDS FOR A `spawn` WHOSE SHAPE IS NOT `<Type>.<factory>(…)` AT ALL** — a bare `spawn f()`, a
// `spawn x.y` with no call. It shares `SpawnTeachingSentence` with the verdicts above rather than restating
// it, because it is the same lesson reached one step earlier: the token shape never named a static.
//
// ⚠ **A BARE `spawn f()` IS REFUSED ON PURPOSE AND PERMANENTLY, not because it is unbuilt.** ⚖ USER RULING
// 2026-08-27: `spawn` is for SERVICES ONLY — there is no unstructured green thread in this language, and
// P2.6's pass fan-out becomes a worker SERVICE rather than a second primitive. That is why this is E3134
// (this is not what `spawn` means) and not E2015 (not built yet).
module function spawnShapeRefusalMessage(written String) returns String
	return "`spawn {written}…` does not start a service: `spawn` is followed by a STATIC CALL on a type, and there is no bare `spawn f()` green thread — the unit of concurrency is a service, whose message surface the compiler can check. {SpawnTeachingSentence}"
end 'spawnShapeRefusalMessage'

// ⭐⭐ **THE WORDS FOR A ROSTER THAT CANNOT BE DISPATCHED — and both of them NAME THE SPAWN by being raised
// at it.** The design's own requirement is that a service diagnostic name the site that made the type a
// service, because whether a type is a service is a whole-program property and the method it fires on may be
// in another file entirely. Raising at the spawn site satisfies it without carrying a site around.
module function serviceRosterRefusalMessage(verdict ServiceRosterVerdict, typeName ByteArray) returns String
	match verdict 'why'
		rosterIsDispatchable then panic("serviceRosterRefusalMessage: asked for the words of a DISPATCHABLE roster on `type {String.from(typeName)}` — the caller refuses only the other arms")
		messageIsOverloaded(methodName, declarationCount) then return "`{String.from(typeName)}.{String.from(methodName)}` is a message of the service `{String.from(typeName)}` and is declared {declarationCount} times. A message becomes ONE variant of the synthesized `{String.from(serviceRequestCompanionName(typeName))}` union, and one variant carries one payload shape — so an overloaded message has no single shape to become. Give the overloads distinct names"
		messageParameterIsNotTransferable(methodName, paramName, noun) then return "parameter `{String.from(paramName)}` of the message `{String.from(typeName)}.{String.from(methodName)}` is {noun}, and this `spawn` makes `{String.from(typeName)}` a service — whose messages MOVE their arguments to another green thread. Send a `.clone()`, send the scalar it is derived from, or drop the parameter from the message"
	end 'why'
end 'serviceRosterRefusalMessage'

// ⭐⭐ **MINT `<T>.request` AND `<T>.handle`, INTO THE INTERNER THE CALLER'S IDS ARE MINTED AGAINST.**
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE INTERNER IS A PARAMETER AND IT IS NOT OPTIONAL — THIS IS OPEN #56's HAZARD (four times, and
// counting).** A payload type built here is a `MaxonType`, which carries a `TypeNameId` that is only a
// number without its table: the layouts the SIGNATURE INDEX holds carry the index's ids, and a
// `FileParseArtifact`'s carry that file's own. `ProgramSignatures.indexTypeNames`' header records what
// resolving one against the wrong table cost — a destructor synthesized for `InstantNanos` on a field that
// is a struct — and `fieldTypeOf`'s records the two doors that each got it wrong once. So the builder never
// picks: every caller hands it the table its own ids live in.
function buildServiceCompanions(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeName ByteArray, into TypeNameInterner) returns ServiceCompanions
	return ServiceCompanions.create(buildServiceRequestUnion(signatures, typeName: typeName, into: into), handle: buildServiceHandleStruct(signatures, typeName: typeName))
end 'buildServiceCompanions'

// The request union: `__shutdown` at variant 0, then one variant per message in declaration order.
function buildServiceRequestUnion(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeName ByteArray, into TypeNameInterner) returns EnumLayout
	var layout = spawnTargetLayoutOf(signatures, typeName: typeName)
	let declFilePath = layout.declFilePath
	var request = EnumLayout.create(serviceRequestCompanionName(typeName), isUnion: true, conformsTo: sharedEmptyDeclaredConformances, visibility: layout.visibility, declFilePath: declFilePath, backing: EnumBacking.integer)

	// ⭐ **VARIANT 0 IS THE COMPILER'S, SO ADDING A MESSAGE NEVER RENUMBERS IT** — see
	// `ServiceShutdownCaseName`. It always carries a reply slot: `shutdown()` is the one message whose
	// completion an author can await, and a graceful drain that could not be waited on would be a race
	// written into the language.
	var shutdownPayloads = PayloadFieldArray.create()
	shutdownPayloads.push(PayloadField.create(ServiceReplySlotName, declaredType: MaxonType.integer, declaredSurface: sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface))
	addServiceRequestCase(request, caseName: ServiceShutdownCaseName, payloads: shutdownPayloads)

	for i in 0 upto layout.messageMethodCount() 'eachMessage'
		let methodName = layout.messageMethodAt(i)
		addServiceRequestCase(request, caseName: methodName, payloads: serviceRequestPayloads(signatures, typeName: typeName, methodName: methodName, declFilePath: declFilePath, into: into))
	end 'eachMessage'

	return request
end 'buildServiceRequestUnion'

// One message's payload list: its parameters as declared, then the reply slot when the message HAS a reply
// to deliver. A message that returns nothing and throws nothing is fire-and-forget — nothing waits on it, so
// there is no cell to carry and no slot to make one for.
function serviceRequestPayloads(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeName ByteArray, methodName ByteArray, declFilePath FilePath, into TypeNameInterner) returns PayloadFieldArray
	let key = qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: methodName)
	let decls = signatures.overloadedDeclsOf(key)
	let decl = try decls.get(0) otherwise panic("serviceRequestPayloads: '{String.from(key)}' is on the roster the declaration sweep built from its own declaration, so `overloadedDeclsOf` cannot be empty")

	var payloads = PayloadFieldArray.create()
	for p in 0 upto decl.paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
		let declared = try decl.paramTypes.get(p) otherwise panic("serviceRequestPayloads: paramTypes.get OOB at {p} below count {decl.paramTypes.count()} for '{String.from(key)}'")
		payloads.push(PayloadField.create(serviceParamName(decl, index: p), declaredType: signatures.declaredSlotType(declared, into: into, slotScopeFilePath: declFilePath), declaredSurface: sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface))
	end 'eachParam'

	if serviceMessageHasAReply(signatures, key: key, decl: decl) 'awaitable'
		payloads.push(PayloadField.create(ServiceReplySlotName, declaredType: MaxonType.integer, declaredSurface: sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface))
	end 'awaitable'

	return payloads
end 'serviceRequestPayloads'

// Does this message deliver a reply? A value-returning message obviously does; a THROWING one does too, and
// that is the half a `returns`-only test would miss — `try await h.wipe()` on a `function wipe() throws E`
// has an error to carry back and nothing else.
function serviceMessageHasAReply(signatures ProgramSignatures, key ByteArray, decl OverloadedDecl) returns bool
	if not maxonReturnTypeIsVoid(decl.returnType) 'returnsAValue'
		return true
	end 'returnsAValue'

	return match signatures.throwsOf(key) 'throws'
		none gives false
		throwing gives true
	end 'throws'
end 'serviceMessageHasAReply'

// Append one variant with the AUTO-INCREMENT tag its declaration position gives it. The tag is DERIVED from
// the case count rather than written down, exactly as `Project.addBuiltinEnumCase` derives a builtin error
// enum's — a literal here would be a second statement of the position and the two are free to disagree.
function addServiceRequestCase(request EnumLayout, caseName ByteArray, payloads PayloadFieldArray)
	let tag = request.caseCount()
	request.addCase(caseName, tag: tag, rawValue: "{tag}", declaresRawValue: false, payloads: payloads, backingFields: sharedEmptyEnumBackingFields)
end 'addServiceRequestCase'

// The handle: ONE field holding the mailbox pointer. On `SignatureIndex.managedFileLayout`'s shape, and for
// its reason — a nominal identity over a single machine word, so a handle is an ordinary box everywhere.
//
// ⚠ **IT CARRIES THE SERVICE'S OWN VISIBILITY AND DECLARING FILE, not `createCompilerOwned`'s `global`**,
// on `appendUnionCasesCompanion`'s precedent: the companion is nameable exactly where the type is, so a
// `module type Calc` does not leak its handle across the module boundary.
function buildServiceHandleStruct(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeName ByteArray) returns StructLayout
	let owner = spawnTargetLayoutOf(signatures, typeName: typeName)
	var handle = StructLayout.create(serviceHandleCompanionName(typeName), visibility: owner.visibility, declFilePath: owner.declFilePath)
	handle.addField(ServiceHandleMailboxFieldName, declaredType: MaxonType.integer, mutable: true, tier: Visibility.file, defaultValue: FieldDefault.none, declaredSurface: sharedEmptyDeclaredSurface)
	return handle
end 'buildServiceHandleStruct'

// ⭐⭐ **THE PERFORM HALF — every candidate the discovery walk noted, classified once and minted once.**
// Called from `Queries.queryProgramSignatures` directly after `internWellKnownContainerInstances()` and
// BEFORE `allFilesFolded = true`, so that:
//   • every whole-program normalization after that line (`rewriteRecordedReturnTypes`,
//     `normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes`, `deriveInstanceNames`) treats the companions as ordinary layouts, and
//   • the decision stands on the FAR SIDE of the stdlib/user name contest, which the fold has settled.
//
// It registers through `ProgramSignatures.recordServiceCompanions`, which is `recordStruct`/`recordEnum` —
// so the companions ride the SAME rolling declaration hash every author-written type does. That is what
// makes the memo correct across files with no separate mix-in: a `spawn` added in file B changes the source
// hash that keys this whole sweep, the sweep mints a companion here, `recordStruct` folds its name and shape
// into `signatures.hash`, and `Queries.currentParseMemoKey` therefore invalidates file A's parse — which is
// where `Calc` is declared and where a reference to `Calc.handle` resolves.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT IS COLLECT-THEN-APPLY, AND THAT IS FORCED BY THE BORROW RULE RATHER THAN CHOSEN.** Minting inside
// the walk mutates `signatures` while `candidate` is an element BORROWED out of it:
// **`E3070: cannot mutate 'signatures' via 'recordServiceCompanions' while it is borrowed by 'candidate'`**.
// ⚠ **MEASURED, and only by shv2 COMPILING ITSELF** — the bootstrap has no such check, so the loop built
// clean at stage-1 and the whole 6,799-case suite was green over it. It is the same shape
// `ProgramSignatures.rewriteRecordedReturnTypes` states once for all three of its rules (a pass that
// rewrites the very map it walks collects first), and the same one `moveOverloadedDecls` records a measured
// USE-AFTER-FREE for: *"the binding read out of the map is a BORROW … 36 spec tests died `nil pointer or
// invalid memory access`"*. Copying the names first ends the aliasing before any write happens.
module function synthesizeServiceCompanions(signatures ProgramSignatures)
	var services = ByteArrayArray.create()
	for candidate in signatures.serviceSpawnCandidates() 'eachCandidate'
		match classifyServiceTarget(signatures, typeName: candidate.typeName, member: candidate.member) 'verdict'
			// Refused at the `spawn` by `Parser.parseSpawn`, which asks this same classification. Nothing is
			// minted here for any of them — see this file's header for why the sweep never reports.
			typeIsNotAStruct to typeIsGeneric then continue
			startsAService then break 'verdict'
		end 'verdict'

		services.push(candidate.typeName)
	end 'eachCandidate'

	for typeName in services 'eachService'
		// Two `spawn`s of one type through two different factories are two CANDIDATES and one service, so the
		// second finds its companions already registered. Asked of the registry rather than of a set built
		// here, because the registry is what a second mint would actually collide with.
		if signatures.containsStruct(serviceHandleCompanionName(typeName)) 'alreadyMinted'
			continue
		end 'alreadyMinted'

		signatures.recordServiceCompanions(buildServiceCompanions(signatures, typeName: typeName, into: signatures.indexTypeNames()))
	end 'eachService'
end 'synthesizeServiceCompanions'
